  }))), "Error analysis"), mdx("p", null, "To understand where the model struggles, I analyzed its predictions on the held-out validation set using the tuned threshold of 0.45. The classification report shows that the model performs very well on label 0 (different authors) with a recall of 0.96 and an F1 of 0.90, but recall for label 1 (same author) drops to 0.37. This indicates that most of the errors come from misclassifying same-author pairs as different authors. The confusion matrix supports this pattern, showing many false negatives for label 1.\nFalse negative errors (same author predicted different) occur when the two spans written by the same author differ strongly in topic, pacing, or tone. For example, one span may contain fast, dialogue-heavy writing while the other is slower and descriptive. Even though the writing comes from the same person, the surface style shifts enough that the model treats them as unrelated. This explains the low recall for label 1.\nFalse Positives errors (different authors predicted same) occur when different-author pairs share similar narrative structure or vocabulary. For instance, two authors may both use short sentences or repeated descriptive phrases typical of fiction writing. The TF-IDF similarity and semantic embeddings capture these overlaps, which leads the model to predict \u201Csame author\u201D even when the texts come from different writers."), mdx("h2", {
